# S18 checkpoint vs. the console's destroy button - 2026-09-12
#
# The human authorised adding 'destroy' to the allowlist once the root target existed (the console
# already had the button; the Makefile had no target for it). This records the frozen file's
# failure with 11 expected names against a Makefile that prints 12, then the same file's PASS
# once the expected array was updated - the assertion code unchanged.

--- BEFORE: scripts/checks/S18.sh with its 11-name TARGETS array, Makefile printing 12

$ bash scripts/checks/S18.sh
  ok: all 11 targets defined
FAIL: make targets disagrees with the allowlist
  listed:
demo-redeploy
demo-undeploy
demo-up
destroy
jit-down
jit-up
jit-verify
ns-delete
state
targets
test-up
verify
  expected:
demo-redeploy
demo-undeploy
demo-up
jit-down
jit-up
jit-verify
ns-delete
state
targets
test-up
verify
exit=1

--- AFTER: the same file with 'destroy' added to its TARGETS array

$ bash scripts/checks/S18.sh
  ok: all 12 targets defined
  ok: make targets prints the allowlist and nothing else
  ok: make state emits the documented JSON
  ok: claim count matches kubectl (3)
  ok: every reported phase matches the cluster
  ok: container count matches docker (3)
  ok: make state degrades to up=false instead of erroring
  ok: ns-delete refuses a missing or unlisted namespace
  ok: a failing target exits non-zero
  ok: demo-undeploy orphans with an expiry, keeps redis Ready, destroys nothing
  ok: demo-redeploy returns every claim to Ready with the expiry cleared
  ok: each run left its log in docs/evidence/
PASS
exit=0
